    {"id":"q","kind":"question","claim":"How does the maximum-modulus step turn the bound on the unit circle into a bound throughout the disk?","authority":"reader-question","evidence":["blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B6","candidate:se-math-2665625"]},
    {"id":"answer","kind":"answer","claim":"For the chosen interior point the polynomial boundary lemma supplies a circle point with no smaller modulus, and the reflection identity bounds the value at every such circle point by the original disk norm; transitivity gives the interior bound.","authority":"secondary","evidence":["blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B5","blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B6","blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B7","lean:Main.lean#hqu"]},
    {"id":"dominating-boundary-point","kind":"premise","claim":"There is a point v with ‖v‖ = 1 and ‖(p.reflect N).eval (1/w)‖ ≤ ‖(p.reflect N).eval v‖.","authority":"formal-proof","evidence":["blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B6","lean:Main.lean#apm_a99J07_poly_le_boundary"]},
    {"id":"circle-bound","kind":"premise","claim":"At that boundary point, ‖(p.reflect N).eval v‖ is at most the unit-disk supremum norm of p.","authority":"formal-proof","evidence":["blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B5","lean:Main.lean#hvb"]},
    {"id":"transitivity-warrant","kind":"warrant","claim":"Transitivity of the two inequalities carries the boundary estimate back to the selected interior point.","authority":"formal-proof","evidence":["blueprint-alignment:a99J07:B7","lean:Main.lean#hqu"]},
